WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.038
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4522.

2
00:00:01.038 --> 00:00:02.077
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.077 --> 00:00:02.423
<v JENNIFER CRAMER>Alright.

4
00:00:02.423 --> 00:00:03.808
Thank you so much.

5
00:00:03.808 --> 00:00:06.923
Okay so today is September twenty-fourth the year 2015,

6
00:00:06.923 --> 00:00:14.192
and this is Jennifer Abraham Cramer with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

7
00:00:14.192 --> 00:00:17.308
And I'm here today with Edward Butch Lemelle Junior.

8
00:00:17.308 --> 00:00:18.000
<v EDWARD LEMELLE JUNIOR>Correct.

9
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:26.377
<v CRAMER>And we are here doing your oral history number one in a series of interviews that we'll be doing a little bit later.

10
00:00:26.377 --> 00:00:28.199
So this is the end.

11
00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:33.662
We are the tail last, very last interview of a very successful oral history day.

12
00:00:33.662 --> 00:00:38.762
The third one that we've had here at the Rigmaiden Center in Mossville, Louisiana.

13
00:00:38.762 --> 00:00:52.603
And we're very grateful to be sitting down with you and you were . . . you and your whole family were very . . . Have been very instrumental in these oral history days and we really appreciate your efforts to that end.

14
00:00:52.603 --> 00:00:57.702
<v LEMELLE>I think my family most of all is very pleased in what you're doing.

15
00:00:57.702 --> 00:01:02.437
And would . . . I'm just wishing that everyone would just join in and participate.

16
00:01:02.437 --> 00:01:06.808
Which I think we’re on a good start though since we started.

17
00:01:06.808 --> 00:01:08.629
<v CRAMER>We're on a great start.

18
00:01:08.629 --> 00:01:11.907
We've got about forty hours of interviews so far.

19
00:01:11.907 --> 00:01:13.000
<v LEMELLE>That's great.

20
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.935
<v CRAMER>Yeah, and after today we probably have about forty-five.

21
00:01:15.935 --> 00:01:17.891
<v LEMELLE>And you got a great staff.

22
00:01:17.891 --> 00:01:19.522
<v CRAMER>We have an exceptional staff.

23
00:01:19.522 --> 00:01:21.152
Couldn't do it without them.

24
00:01:21.152 --> 00:01:21.478
<v LEMELLE>Right.

25
00:01:21.478 --> 00:01:21.804
Right.

26
00:01:21.804 --> 00:01:22.457
<v CRAMER>They're amazing.

27
00:01:22.457 --> 00:01:24.413
And everybody's really passionate about it.

28
00:01:24.413 --> 00:01:28.000
Everybody really loves it and looks forward to doing it.

29
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.181
<v LEMELLE>And we really, really . . . I know from my heart I appreciate it.

30
00:01:32.181 --> 00:01:39.845
I really appreciate it because I always said when this first came about, what would my grandkids and great-grandkids know about Mossville?

31
00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:45.419
Because several years ago we were so happy when they start putting it on the map.

32
00:01:45.419 --> 00:01:49.600
The name was on the . . . They never had Mossville on the map.

33
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:55.174
I guess about fifteen or twenty years ago they start putting the little bitty fine letters,

34
00:01:55.174 --> 00:02:00.400
Lake Charles [Louisiana], Westlake [Louisiana], and they had a little thing that say Sulphur [Louisiana].

35
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:01.445
And you . . . Mossville!

36
00:02:01.445 --> 00:02:02.490
Where is that?

37
00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:11.548
So I mark . . . my kids always . . . Grandkids especially want to know a lot about Mossville because I tell them some stories and they can't believe it.

38
00:02:11.548 --> 00:02:13.290
<v CRAMER>Well that's why we're here.

39
00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:15.381
We're getting it all on tape.

40
00:02:15.381 --> 00:02:18.168
And this is . . . Or well I say tape.

41
00:02:18.168 --> 00:02:19.213
That dates me.

42
00:02:19.213 --> 00:02:21.303
We're getting it recorded for posterity.

43
00:02:21.303 --> 00:02:22.000
<v LEMELLE>Recorded.

44
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:22.843
<v CRAMER>Yes, indeed.

45
00:02:22.843 --> 00:02:28.325
Okay so we're going to start with when and where were you born?

46
00:02:28.325 --> 00:02:37.602
<v LEMELLE>I was born in the city of Lake Charles, but my parents are Edward and Ida May Lemelle, was residents of Mossville.

47
00:02:37.602 --> 00:02:44.349
But I was one of the fortunate kids born in Saint Patrick or either Stevenson's Clinic,

48
00:02:44.349 --> 00:02:48.988
and then that way I was brought back home to Mossville.

49
00:02:48.988 --> 00:02:51.940
But this has always been my home.

50
00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.892
I was born February the fourth, 1946.

51
00:02:54.892 --> 00:02:58.265
And what was so exciting about that year?

52
00:02:58.265 --> 00:03:05.434
If you all recall history in 1945, they had a thing called the ending of a war.

53
00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:13.867
And in 1946 a child was born. [Laughter] And I was . . . Being the only son my mother and father had,

54
00:03:13.867 --> 00:03:27.361
I have five sisters and my mother and father were so . . . I guess that's why I'm . . . I got everybody spoiled because I was the baby and the only boy in the family.

55
00:03:27.361 --> 00:03:34.530
So being born like I said 1946, and came home, this has been my home day one.

56
00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:40.855
Of course, I moved away several times, but found myself back with the pine trees.

57
00:03:40.855 --> 00:03:42.964
There's no place like home.

58
00:03:42.964 --> 00:03:49.289
Never had to click the heels together, but I always found my way back home.

59
00:03:49.289 --> 00:03:56.880
And I always say it's been one of the best places I think in the world to live,

60
00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:04.470
because I've lived on the east coast, west coast, and in between and there's no place like Mossville.

61
00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.000
I can grant you that.

62
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.124
<v CRAMER>You were one of the very first baby boom generation.

63
00:04:11.124 --> 00:04:12.773
<v LEMELLE>Very first baby booms.

64
00:04:12.773 --> 00:04:20.608
This is a good joke I tell people, because I still have kids that still come living with me.

65
00:04:20.608 --> 00:04:25.557
My kids are living . . . I said they call us the baby boomers.

66
00:04:25.557 --> 00:04:32.979
But I think they should have called this generation the boomerangs because all of them come back home.

67
00:04:32.979 --> 00:04:33.392
<v CRAMER>[Laughs].

68
00:04:33.392 --> 00:04:42.052
<v LEMELLE>And I'm not the only one in the area that . . . Our kids, they end up coming back to Mommy and Daddy.

69
00:04:42.052 --> 00:04:42.464
<v CRAMER>Yes.

70
00:04:42.464 --> 00:04:45.763
<v LEMELLE>And . . . But one of the first baby boomers.

71
00:04:45.763 --> 00:04:47.000
Yes, ma'am.

72
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.258
<v CRAMER>And you're . . . You're Julia's brother?

73
00:04:49.258 --> 00:04:51.968
<v LEMELLE>I'm Edward . . . Don't let her . . . Please . . .

74
00:04:51.968 --> 00:04:53.774
<v CRAMER>Oh Ms. Edward Julia.

75
00:04:53.774 --> 00:04:56.484
<v LEMELLE>Please, her name is Edward Julia.

76
00:04:56.484 --> 00:04:57.839
She's my sister.

77
00:04:57.839 --> 00:05:01.000
She's nine years older than me.

78
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:01.333
<v CRAMER>Right.

79
00:05:01.333 --> 00:05:05.667
So your dad . . . he . . . I remember her talking about how he spoke French.

80
00:05:05.667 --> 00:05:06.333
<v LEMELLE>He was.

81
00:05:06.333 --> 00:05:07.333
<v CRAMER>Or he was . . .?

82
00:05:07.333 --> 00:05:08.667
<v LEMELLE>He wouldn't teach us.

83
00:05:08.667 --> 00:05:11.000
<v CRAMER>Why do you think that is?

84
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:22.642
<v LEMELLE>Because he started off with bad words and my mama would . . . Dared him to let us speak French because he told us a few little bad words.

85
00:05:22.642 --> 00:05:25.229
Now Julia's son, he taught him.

86
00:05:25.229 --> 00:05:31.266
He's now in Paris, but my father helped him with a lot of French.

87
00:05:31.266 --> 00:05:38.165
But my mother never would let my dad because he was more of a comical guy.

88
00:05:38.165 --> 00:05:45.927
A jokester, you know, liked to have fun and some of those little words he would tell us.

89
00:05:45.927 --> 00:05:52.394
Mama'd say, "Boy, I catch you, I'm going to wash your mouth out with soap."

90
00:05:52.394 --> 00:05:56.275
<v CRAMER>So tell us a little bit about your grandparents.

91
00:05:56.275 --> 00:06:02.312
What their names were on your mom's side and then on your dad's side?

92
00:06:02.312 --> 00:06:07.486
<v LEMELLE>My mother's father mother . . . I could even go to my mother's great-grandfather.

93
00:06:07.486 --> 00:06:13.954
But we'll start at my mother's mother was Ira Pots who married a David Duff

94
00:06:13.954 --> 00:06:14.385
<c.p>Towner.

95
00:06:14.385 --> 00:06:16.110
Back in . . . What year?

96
00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:17.404
I don't know.

97
00:06:17.404 --> 00:06:20.853
But that was my mother and father's parents.

98
00:06:20.853 --> 00:06:24.734
And my mother was the second of eleven children.

99
00:06:24.734 --> 00:06:29.046
She was the second born to Ira and David Towner.

100
00:06:29.046 --> 00:06:38.532
And my mother was born . . . So to kind of get you an idea, my mother was born in December the sixth, 1903.

101
00:06:38.532 --> 00:06:45.000
So that kind of gives you an idea of where the family come from.

102
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:56.263
And just to give you a little history my grandfather . . . My mother said when she was a little girl my grandfather was the son of a former slave,

103
00:06:56.263 --> 00:07:03.101
and they were living somewhere in a little place called Kipling, Louisiana somewhere around Sugartown, DeRidder [Louisiana].

104
00:07:03.101 --> 00:07:06.721
And they migrated this way with horse and wagon.

105
00:07:06.721 --> 00:07:15.570
She was a little girl because she said they had . . . It had taken them three days to get from there to here.

106
00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:25.626
So you know that was a . . . She said she could remember sleeping under the stars until they eventually made it here to the Mossville area.

107
00:07:25.626 --> 00:07:31.257
Which now a lot of that . . . The old Evergreen Road up in that area,

108
00:07:31.257 --> 00:07:40.911
what now belongs to Sasol [energy and chemical company], was my mother and father's first home that they had and their home burnt down.

109
00:07:40.911 --> 00:07:48.955
From what my mother says she was a little girl, and they moved up here on the Old Spanish Trail.

110
00:07:48.955 --> 00:07:52.173
Great, great . . . I had some great, wonderful grandparents.

111
00:07:52.173 --> 00:07:57.000
Lord, they could whip good but they loved you to death.

112
00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.479
<v CRAMER>What brought them here to Mossville?

113
00:07:59.479 --> 00:08:01.132
Why did they move?

114
00:08:01.132 --> 00:08:09.397
<v LEMELLE>Well I . . . Like I said, my grandfather from the stories they told us was the son of a former slave.

115
00:08:09.397 --> 00:08:13.942
And he had taken on that name of a slave owner.

116
00:08:13.942 --> 00:08:30.058
And when he was allowed to leave from that area . . . What reason they came this way I think because it . . . Back in those days things were changing as far as work and construction and people building in the areas.

117
00:08:30.058 --> 00:08:30.471
And

118
00:08:30.471 --> 00:08:36.669
<c.p>that caused him to just . . . I guess more or less you'd say a better life.

119
00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:39.975
Getting off the farms and all those things.

120
00:08:39.975 --> 00:08:47.000
And moving to a place that seemed to be more free than where he was at.

121
00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:51.189
<v CRAMER>What kind of work did he get when he got here?

122
00:08:51.189 --> 00:08:54.616
<v LEMELLE>My grandfather was more or less a self-employed person.

123
00:08:54.616 --> 00:09:01.852
He did a lot of things for himself, and my grandmother was a carpenter, she could do carpentry work.

124
00:09:01.852 --> 00:09:11.753
But my grandfather . . . What I can remember some of things that he did was like in the wood . . . He would do a lot of timber work,

125
00:09:11.753 --> 00:09:16.703
gardening, raise animals, and he used to take in . . . He actually sold coal.

126
00:09:16.703 --> 00:09:23.939
He would take different types of wood and he would burn it until it got down to a coal.

127
00:09:23.939 --> 00:09:28.128
He would cool it and the coal was used in homes.

128
00:09:28.128 --> 00:09:31.555
He either sold it or gave it to people.

129
00:09:31.555 --> 00:09:34.983
That's what they burned . . . The coal heaters and stuff.

130
00:09:34.983 --> 00:09:41.837
But he actually had a way that he did . . . He tried to teach us but I don’t know,

131
00:09:41.837 --> 00:09:44.122
that was too hard for me.

132
00:09:44.122 --> 00:09:47.930
He actually did a lot of stuff like that, gardening.

133
00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:51.738
And I can remember that a lot about my grandfather.

134
00:09:51.738 --> 00:09:54.404
The way he was a great gardener.

135
00:09:54.404 --> 00:10:02.782
My grandmother, now she was a . . . She could build a chicken coop, or a house, or whatever you wanted in an instant.

136
00:10:02.782 --> 00:10:07.733
And my grandfather was more or less the person that was the inside.

137
00:10:07.733 --> 00:10:16.872
He did the cooking and watching the kids and Grandmother would be doing . . . And from that my mother's . . . Had four brothers and all three,

138
00:10:16.872 --> 00:10:19.538
taking after their mother, was professional carpenters.

139
00:10:19.538 --> 00:10:24.488
They could build a . . . They would get together; four guys would get together.

140
00:10:24.488 --> 00:10:26.773
They would put up the house.

141
00:10:26.773 --> 00:10:29.058
One could frame and box in.

142
00:10:29.058 --> 00:10:32.105
One was an electrician and did hard work.

143
00:10:32.105 --> 00:10:39.721
The other one was a . . . In fact when we go to my mother's house I'm going to get a key,

144
00:10:39.721 --> 00:10:47.718
and I'm going to go in, and I'm going to show you some cabinets that her brother built probably in 1960.

145
00:10:47.718 --> 00:10:50.384
<c.p>And they still in the old house.

146
00:10:50.384 --> 00:10:58.000
So you know, that's all I can remember so much about my grandmother and grandfather on my mother's side.

147
00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:01.265
<v CRAMER>Do you know where she learned the carpentry skills?

148
00:11:01.265 --> 00:11:02.353
Who taught her?

149
00:11:02.353 --> 00:11:04.892
Did she talk about that at all?

150
00:11:04.892 --> 00:11:06.343
<v LEMELLE>More or less herself.

151
00:11:06.343 --> 00:11:14.324
And like you said, some of these things I could . . . When we get ready, because some things in the family that’s hush-hush.

152
00:11:14.324 --> 00:11:21.216
My grandmother was a . . . Actually her mother was a slave and she was the child of a slave owner.

153
00:11:21.216 --> 00:11:28.833
And she never wanted us to talk . . . Because he was in Irishman and she had a lot of Irish in her,

154
00:11:28.833 --> 00:11:32.098
and when you see the pictures you'll see it.

155
00:11:32.098 --> 00:11:35.000
That she was fair fair with green eyes.

156
00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:53.863
And I think that's where she got a lot of her skills because that's a lot of things that they did in construction and building when she was a little girl back . . . But some of those things like the family they . . . I had an auntie she slap you if you ask her,

157
00:11:53.863 --> 00:11:56.039
"What was grandma's daddy was named?"

158
00:11:56.039 --> 00:11:57.127
She'd slap you.

159
00:11:57.127 --> 00:12:00.392
"Boy, you don't need to be talking about that."

160
00:12:00.392 --> 00:12:08.735
So that's why I think . . . I know that's where she got a lot of her carpentry skills . . . Learning how to do things out,

161
00:12:08.735 --> 00:12:12.000
because this was what they had to do.

162
00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:16.588
<v CRAMER>Where did . . . Where was your mom . . . Where was she before they came here?

163
00:12:16.588 --> 00:12:20.118
I think you told me that, but I can't remember.

164
00:12:20.118 --> 00:12:20.824
<v LEMELLE>My grandmother?

165
00:12:20.824 --> 00:12:21.176
<v CRAMER>Yes.

166
00:12:21.176 --> 00:12:22.235
Where was she?

167
00:12:22.235 --> 00:12:24.000
<v LEMELLE>This was around Kipling.

168
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:24.455
<v CRAMER>Kipling.

169
00:12:24.455 --> 00:12:24.909
<v LEMELLE>Some . . .

170
00:12:24.909 --> 00:12:25.364
<v CRAMER>Where?

171
00:12:25.364 --> 00:12:26.727
Where is Kipling?

172
00:12:26.727 --> 00:12:29.909
<v LEMELLE>Kipling is a few miles from Sugartown.

173
00:12:29.909 --> 00:12:33.091
The area of Sugartown in DeRidder, Louisiana.

174
00:12:33.091 --> 00:12:34.000
<v CRAMER>Okay.

175
00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:37.229
<v LEMELLE>It was just another little . . . Say like Lake Charles, Westlake, Mossville.

176
00:12:37.229 --> 00:12:42.514
It was just a little place that they call . . . Had a different name as from DeRidder or Sugartown.

177
00:12:42.514 --> 00:12:47.211
My mama always said it was from Kipling, and some said no, they was from Sugartown.

178
00:12:47.211 --> 00:12:54.550
So I think they were kind of in between where they lived at because I think the old people went from one farm to another.

179
00:12:54.550 --> 00:13:00.128
He might have been here for cotton season and over here for something else before he migrated this way.

180
00:13:00.128 --> 00:13:04.239
<v CRAMER>So did he work for the lumber industry at all when he got here?

181
00:13:04.239 --> 00:13:04.532
<v LEMELLE>No.

182
00:13:04.532 --> 00:13:04.826
<v CRAMER>Okay.

183
00:13:04.826 --> 00:13:06.000
Not at all.

184
00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.724
<v LEMELLE>My grandfather was more self-employed.

185
00:13:07.724 --> 00:13:08.759
<v CRAMER>Like he's self-employed?

186
00:13:08.759 --> 00:13:09.103
<v LEMELLE>Right.

187
00:13:09.103 --> 00:13:16.000
<v CRAMER>And so what was . . . When he was working for himself, what are some of the things that he did?

188
00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:18.850
<v LEMELLE>That's what I was telling you a minute ago.

189
00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:23.600
He did a lot of lumber, but not as far as working in a mill.

190
00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:27.717
And a lot of his skills came from, like I say, from gardening.

191
00:13:27.717 --> 00:13:31.200
And I know he sold a lot, a lot of coals.

192
00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:31.833
<v CRAMER>That's right.

193
00:13:31.833 --> 00:13:33.417
Where did he learn that?

194
00:13:33.417 --> 00:13:35.000
Where did he learn . . .?

195
00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:36.187
<v LEMELLE>I have no idea.

196
00:13:36.187 --> 00:13:37.374
<v CRAMER>Did he talk about?

197
00:13:37.374 --> 00:13:42.418
Because you said he tried to teach ya’ll how to do it and ya’ll are like, "No."

198
00:13:42.418 --> 00:13:45.088
<v LEMELLE>I have no idea where picked that up at.

199
00:13:45.088 --> 00:13:45.385
<v CRAMER>Yeah.

200
00:13:45.385 --> 00:13:45.978
<v LEMELLE>And why.

201
00:13:45.978 --> 00:13:51.319
But again, like I say, my grandfather was more . . . After that he was more of an inside man.

202
00:13:51.319 --> 00:13:53.989
He did most . . . Because Julia said Grandmother could cook.

203
00:13:53.989 --> 00:13:56.066
I said, “Man, my grandma couldn’t cook.

204
00:13:56.066 --> 00:13:57.549
Grandpa did all the cooking.”

205
00:13:57.549 --> 00:14:02.000
So he was more like the house man that I can remember about him.

206
00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.291
<v CRAMER>So when we come back for a further interview,

207
00:14:04.291 --> 00:14:08.873
I do want to ask you about his garden and anything you might remember about what he gardened,

208
00:14:08.873 --> 00:14:12.182
and if he gardened by the moon, and all that kind of stuff.

209
00:14:12.182 --> 00:14:14.473
So I do want to get back with that.

210
00:14:14.473 --> 00:14:15.491
<v LEMELLE>Definitely by the moon.

211
00:14:15.491 --> 00:14:16.000
<v CRAMER>Right.

212
00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:18.687
<v LEMELLE>We going to talk about those things,

213
00:14:18.687 --> 00:14:27.899
because back in this community years ago we had the creditors . . . That we didn't have the stores that would handle some of the things.

214
00:14:27.899 --> 00:14:35.192
And we had creditors in Westlake and they would load up their wagons and go down and make groceries.

215
00:14:35.192 --> 00:14:42.485
And my grandfather . . . I can remember so many times that they would have like what we call a butchery.

216
00:14:42.485 --> 00:14:49.778
And he may have had . . . he may . . . They might would get together and butcher six hogs in one day.

217
00:14:49.778 --> 00:14:53.616
And the meat was spread from one end of the

218
00:14:53.616 --> 00:14:55.152
<c.p>community to the other.

219
00:14:55.152 --> 00:14:59.758
And the person out here might have a garden with sweet potatoes.

220
00:14:59.758 --> 00:15:03.212
One might have a garden full off [wash?] potatoes.

221
00:15:03.212 --> 00:15:04.747
And they all shared.

222
00:15:04.747 --> 00:15:08.586
One brought the meat, the other one bring the vegetables.

223
00:15:08.586 --> 00:15:13.576
Because my grandfather, he used to grow a lot, a lot of watermelons.

224
00:15:13.576 --> 00:15:17.798
But he picked that up from Sugartown, how to do that.

225
00:15:17.798 --> 00:15:21.253
That's what he saw them do in that area.

226
00:15:21.253 --> 00:15:25.859
So he was great at . . . My grandfather was just a great gardener.

227
00:15:25.859 --> 00:15:27.778
He had a green thumb.

228
00:15:27.778 --> 00:15:28.545
Like mine.

229
00:15:28.545 --> 00:15:32.000
<v CRAMER>What time of year were the butcheries typically?

230
00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:32.316
<v LEMELLE>November.

231
00:15:32.316 --> 00:15:32.632
December.

232
00:15:32.632 --> 00:15:33.263
<v CRAMER>Right about . . .

233
00:15:33.263 --> 00:15:34.526
<v LEMELLE>Cool . . . The cool weather.

234
00:15:34.526 --> 00:15:34.842
<v CRAMER>Soon?

235
00:15:34.842 --> 00:15:36.105
<v LEMELLE>Right, the cool weather.

236
00:15:36.105 --> 00:15:37.684
They always was cold weather.

237
00:15:37.684 --> 00:15:44.000
Very seldom they did any butchering during the heat, because they didn't want things to waste, the cold weather.

238
00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:46.852
<v CRAMER>So how did they preserve the meat?

239
00:15:46.852 --> 00:15:52.148
<v LEMELLE>I can remember my grandfather . . . He had several ways that he preserved meat,

240
00:15:52.148 --> 00:15:57.444
<c.p>and one way was astonishing to us, because he had in his backyard,

241
00:15:57.444 --> 00:16:04.778
I can remember like yesterday, he dug a hole about six foot deep and about eight foot wide.

242
00:16:04.778 --> 00:16:12.111
And back in those days they had a wagon that would come from Westlake they call the iceman.

243
00:16:12.111 --> 00:16:14.556
But my grandfather dug this deal.

244
00:16:14.556 --> 00:16:19.852
Little old short man . . . He was short in stature, but nothing but man.

245
00:16:19.852 --> 00:16:24.333
And when he dug it he boarded it all up around.

246
00:16:24.333 --> 00:16:30.037
It was more like a basement but it was just out in the yard.

247
00:16:30.037 --> 00:16:34.111
And some of his butchering we’d bring it in there,

248
00:16:34.111 --> 00:16:41.444
then the iceman would come and they put ice down there and wrap it up in some canvas.

249
00:16:41.444 --> 00:16:43.481
The big old thick canvas.

250
00:16:43.481 --> 00:16:45.111
They had a way.

251
00:16:45.111 --> 00:16:50.407
And then they had . . . I believe I still got it, one of them.

252
00:16:50.407 --> 00:16:53.259
They had what they call a crockpots.

253
00:16:53.259 --> 00:17:00.185
They would take the crockpots and salt their meat down and put the meat in the crockpots,

254
00:17:00.185 --> 00:17:05.481
and they'd last look like forever, what we didn't steal out of it.

255
00:17:05.481 --> 00:17:12.000
So that was one way I can remember him preserving a lot of his food.

256
00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:20.136
And canning, that's where they did a lot of canning certain time of the year, but they didn't can like we do now.

257
00:17:20.136 --> 00:17:22.966
They would actually can like okra . . . Different things.

258
00:17:22.966 --> 00:17:26.150
They would can their okra, tomatoes, and all that.

259
00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:30.041
And the way they canned it then they was in jars.

260
00:17:30.041 --> 00:17:36.762
And that jar once they sealed it, you put that jar in the cabinet, it would last five years.

261
00:17:36.762 --> 00:17:39.946
So because at that time they didn't have refrigeration.

262
00:17:39.946 --> 00:17:45.252
And what we . . . Well I guess that’s where we got the name ice box from.

263
00:17:45.252 --> 00:17:57.279
They would come and this guy would bring a block of ice and put some in the outside and then bring some ice and put it in the house in the little old icebox.

264
00:17:57.279 --> 00:17:59.048
It was good days though.

265
00:17:59.048 --> 00:18:02.939
One thing about, it we never went without anything to eat.

266
00:18:02.939 --> 00:18:04.000
<v CRAMER>That's right.

267
00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:04.790
<v LEMELLE>Never, never.

268
00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:10.318
I'm going to tell you a story one day about my . . . I can tell

269
00:18:10.318 --> 00:18:13.083
<c.p>you a story about my grandfather now.

270
00:18:13.083 --> 00:18:14.268
<v CRAMER>Let's do it.

271
00:18:14.268 --> 00:18:18.217
<v LEMELLE>My grandmother was a praying woman who loved her grandkids.

272
00:18:18.217 --> 00:18:22.561
She loved us dear and she always taught us the Bible.

273
00:18:22.561 --> 00:18:26.904
And its two things she taught me that I'll never forget.

274
00:18:26.904 --> 00:18:33.223
She said, "I've never . . ." And I learned this scripture when I was like a little kid.

275
00:18:33.223 --> 00:18:37.172
That, “I've never seen the righteous forsaken, nor her seed.”

276
00:18:37.172 --> 00:18:41.121
She was talking about herself, or her seed bag bread.

277
00:18:41.121 --> 00:18:44.675
She said, "I want you to always remember that."

278
00:18:44.675 --> 00:18:53.758
At that time it didn't mean nothing to me, but as time passed in our life we have never, never went without anything.

279
00:18:53.758 --> 00:18:54.153
Never.

280
00:18:54.153 --> 00:18:59.287
None of her ancestors, I mean her kids up under her; grandkids, great-great-greats.

281
00:18:59.287 --> 00:19:01.656
None of us has ever suffered.

282
00:19:01.656 --> 00:19:06.000
We've always remembered the voice of Grandmother and my grandfather.

283
00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:07.182
Oh, Grandpa Duff.

284
00:19:07.182 --> 00:19:08.759
I can see it.

285
00:19:08.759 --> 00:19:11.124
He was a loving, loving grandfather.

286
00:19:11.124 --> 00:19:13.094
He . . . They just loved us.

287
00:19:13.094 --> 00:19:16.642
And he always taught us about family . . . Family values.

288
00:19:16.642 --> 00:19:24.524
That was so great to us because his thing was, you could choose your friends but your family is yours.

289
00:19:24.524 --> 00:19:27.678
And I have a first cousin named Rogers.

290
00:19:27.678 --> 00:19:31.619
And we got into a . . . This is a true story.

291
00:19:31.619 --> 00:19:36.743
We got into a scrap one day fussing, doing some little bad things.

292
00:19:36.743 --> 00:19:40.290
And my grandfather saw us and he called us.

293
00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:41.078
Picture this.

294
00:19:41.078 --> 00:19:52.114
There's two trees that he had in this yard and they had what we call the little cocklebur tree that was sitting on the side of his house.

295
00:19:52.114 --> 00:19:54.873
A tree here and a tree there.

296
00:19:54.873 --> 00:20:02.362
In between that tree was the bench that he sat on and we had to sit around his feet.

297
00:20:02.362 --> 00:20:04.332
And he would teach us.

298
00:20:04.332 --> 00:20:11.427
So he called my cousin and I over, and these particular trees they call it a gum tree.

299
00:20:11.427 --> 00:20:17.733
They had a little cocklebur, a little ball and it was full of like little stickers.

300
00:20:17.733 --> 00:20:25.616
So he called us over and he made myself pick up one and he had my cousin pick up one.

301
00:20:25.616 --> 00:20:28.375
This is a story I'll never forget.

302
00:20:28.375 --> 00:20:28.769
And

303
00:20:28.769 --> 00:20:33.498
<c.p>he said, "Why you boys out there cutting up with one another?"

304
00:20:33.498 --> 00:20:37.834
Of course we tried to lie our way out of it.

305
00:20:37.834 --> 00:20:41.381
And he said, "Do you see these two trees?"

306
00:20:41.381 --> 00:20:42.564
Said, "Yes, Grandpa."

307
00:20:42.564 --> 00:20:46.505
He said, "This is me and this is your grandmother."

308
00:20:46.505 --> 00:20:51.235
He say, "This bench is like what ties she and I together.

309
00:20:51.235 --> 00:20:52.417
We are one."

310
00:20:52.417 --> 00:20:55.570
And he said, "All of these little cockleburs?

311
00:20:55.570 --> 00:20:56.358
That's y’all."

312
00:20:56.358 --> 00:20:57.935
I never forget it.

313
00:20:57.935 --> 00:21:03.059
And he said, "A family is like . . . Just like all these little cockleburs."

314
00:21:03.059 --> 00:21:07.000
Only he said, "A family is just like that."

315
00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:13.954
And he had me, my cousin, and he’d taken . . . He said, "Now Butch, Rogers, y’all join those burrs together."

316
00:21:13.954 --> 00:21:17.247
And we stuck them together and they stuck together.

317
00:21:17.247 --> 00:21:19.443
He said, "Now turn them loose."

318
00:21:19.443 --> 00:21:22.737
And when we dropped them they stayed hooked together.

319
00:21:22.737 --> 00:21:24.933
He said, "That's what family's about."

320
00:21:24.933 --> 00:21:27.861
He said . . . And he made us pile up.

321
00:21:27.861 --> 00:21:31.887
I mean we had a pile of cockleburs like that high.

322
00:21:31.887 --> 00:21:35.180
He made us get them all up . . . Pile them.

323
00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:36.278
Just started piling.

324
00:21:36.278 --> 00:21:42.500
He said, "This is my . . . This is your family and forever you will always be stuck together."

325
00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:46.892
He was a . . . They just had so many . . . So much [mother width?].

326
00:21:46.892 --> 00:21:48.356
So much mother width.

327
00:21:48.356 --> 00:21:50.552
And mother width and praying people.

328
00:21:50.552 --> 00:21:54.211
That's what . . . That's what got us over where we are.

329
00:21:54.211 --> 00:21:55.675
A great-grandmother and great-grandfather.

330
00:21:55.675 --> 00:21:57.139
And we had it.

331
00:21:57.139 --> 00:22:02.995
And the . . . they had . . . You know they say it takes a village to raise a kid?

332
00:22:02.995 --> 00:22:06.289
This what this was, a village that raise kids.

333
00:22:06.289 --> 00:22:07.021
Yes, ma'am.

334
00:22:07.021 --> 00:22:13.242
So ya’ll can take that out because my grandmother and my grandfather . . . I get emotional about it.

335
00:22:13.242 --> 00:22:15.438
<v CRAMER>I'm . . . You're making me emotional, too.

336
00:22:15.438 --> 00:22:16.170
That's alright.

337
00:22:16.170 --> 00:22:18.000
It's an emotional thing.

338
00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:20.174
<v LEMELLE>And you know what else?

339
00:22:20.174 --> 00:22:21.478
My niece Kim.

340
00:22:21.478 --> 00:22:26.696
She ended up owning the land where my grandmother and grandfather lived.

341
00:22:26.696 --> 00:22:30.609
And dedicated it . . . Or donated it for the church.

342
00:22:30.609 --> 00:22:31.478
<v CRAMER>Which church?

343
00:22:31.478 --> 00:22:32.348
<v LEMELLE>Mossville Truth.

344
00:22:32.348 --> 00:22:35.391
Who has now moved to Lake Charles.

345
00:22:35.391 --> 00:22:38.000
<v CRAMER>Is that Mossville Truth Tabernacle?

346
00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:38.420
<v LEMELLE>Right.

347
00:22:38.420 --> 00:22:46.408
We were there . . . That church was built on my grandmother and grandfather's property that Kim donated for the church.

348
00:22:46.408 --> 00:22:54.816
And we just moved but . . . And another thing about that place, we always said it would be a holy grounds.

349
00:22:54.816 --> 00:23:04.906
Holy, holy because that's what my grandmother said her place would always be a place to feed hungry or . . . Because that's what they did.

350
00:23:04.906 --> 00:23:15.416
And when my pastor was getting ready to demo [demolish] to make ready to build a church, my grandmother and grandfather’s house was still standing.

351
00:23:15.416 --> 00:23:21.722
And he had trucks that was to come and tear down and haul it off.

352
00:23:21.722 --> 00:23:25.927
And that man paced up and down and he couldn't.

353
00:23:25.927 --> 00:23:29.710
He paid those guys, the truckers, he paid them.

354
00:23:29.710 --> 00:23:35.596
He said, "Y’all go ahead on I'm going . . . I got to make another decision."

355
00:23:35.596 --> 00:23:40.641
And he told me he said, "I can't tear this house down.

356
00:23:40.641 --> 00:23:42.743
I can't let it leave."

357
00:23:42.743 --> 00:23:46.947
He went and rented a machine and dug a hole.

358
00:23:46.947 --> 00:23:49.469
My grandmother's house is still there.

359
00:23:49.469 --> 00:23:52.833
He would not let them haul it off.

360
00:23:52.833 --> 00:23:57.878
He said, "Not a nail from this house is going to leave."

361
00:23:57.878 --> 00:23:59.559
So he buried it.

362
00:23:59.559 --> 00:24:02.922
So the holy ground is yet still there.

363
00:24:02.922 --> 00:24:06.706
I don't know what Sasol is going to do.

364
00:24:06.706 --> 00:24:09.229
Somebody might get saved over there.

365
00:24:09.229 --> 00:24:16.796
But it’s been so much with our family man, that's why I didn't want to do this interview.

366
00:24:16.796 --> 00:24:21.000
<v CRAMER>Is that the house that you took me to?

367
00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:21.312
<v LEMELLE>No.

368
00:24:21.312 --> 00:24:21.625
<v CRAMER>Okay.

369
00:24:21.625 --> 00:24:23.188
<v LEMELLE>That's where the church was.

370
00:24:23.188 --> 00:24:26.000
My mother's and them was right across the street.

371
00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:26.312
<v CRAMER>Okay.

372
00:24:26.312 --> 00:24:27.250
<v LEMELLE>The old house.

373
00:24:27.250 --> 00:24:31.000
Yeah my mother's house is across the street from my grandmother.

374
00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:34.805
<v CRAMER>Maybe next time we could maybe draw a little memory map or something.

375
00:24:34.805 --> 00:24:38.902
Like a map where you kind of can point to me where things were.

376
00:24:38.902 --> 00:24:39.195
<v LEMELLE>Right.

377
00:24:39.195 --> 00:24:39.488
Yeah.

378
00:24:39.488 --> 00:24:42.415
<v CRAMER>That way I can maybe get a visual on things.

379
00:24:42.415 --> 00:24:43.000
<v LEMELLE>Right.

380
00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:46.135
<v CRAMER>Is that bench and those trees still there?

381
00:24:46.135 --> 00:24:47.703
Are they still there?

382
00:24:47.703 --> 00:24:49.270
<v LEMELLE>No. All that's gone.

383
00:24:49.270 --> 00:24:52.405
<v CRAMER>Do you have any pictures of that anywhere?

384
00:24:52.405 --> 00:24:53.581
Do you think?

385
00:24:53.581 --> 00:24:57.892
<v LEMELLE>I probably can find some pictures of my grandmother and grandfather's.

386
00:24:57.892 --> 00:25:00.243
I don't know about the trees.

387
00:25:00.243 --> 00:25:10.041
But their fifty year anniversary when we were all small we’ve got . . . I have pictures of that and I'm outside where they had their reception.

388
00:25:10.041 --> 00:25:11.216
Their fifty year.

389
00:25:11.216 --> 00:25:16.703
In fact I have a plate from my grandmother and grandfather's fifty year anniversary.

390
00:25:16.703 --> 00:25:22.973
Then I have, from my mother and father's fifty year, I got some things from them.

391
00:25:22.973 --> 00:25:28.459
And then I have my wife and I twenty-five and waiting on the fifty.

392
00:25:28.459 --> 00:25:30.027
But we have pictures.

393
00:25:30.027 --> 00:25:33.554
I can find some picture of the old place.

394
00:25:33.554 --> 00:25:33.946
<v CRAMER>Okay.

395
00:25:33.946 --> 00:25:37.473
And that was all . . . That was your mother's parents?

396
00:25:37.473 --> 00:25:38.649
<v LEMELLE>My mother's parents.

397
00:25:38.649 --> 00:25:41.000
<v CRAMER>That we just talked about.

398
00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:41.359
<v LEMELLE>Right.

399
00:25:41.359 --> 00:25:43.872
<v CRAMER>That's quite . . . Those were quite some stories.

400
00:25:43.872 --> 00:25:47.821
And then you said your other grandparents were really, really close.

401
00:25:47.821 --> 00:25:49.256
Like right across the . . .?

402
00:25:49.256 --> 00:25:52.487
<v LEMELLE>No this was the close . . . My mother and father . . .

403
00:25:52.487 --> 00:25:52.846
<v CRAMER>Okay.

404
00:25:52.846 --> 00:26:00.385
<v LEMELLE>My father father I knew but his mother, his biological mother, he was only seven years old when she passed away.

405
00:26:00.385 --> 00:26:05.769
Now I knew my other grandmother, but they were . . . They lived in New Iberia [Louisiana].

406
00:26:05.769 --> 00:26:07.205
<v CRAMER>What are their names?

407
00:26:07.205 --> 00:26:09.000
What were their names?

408
00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:11.951
<v LEMELLE>My grandfather's name was [Elwah?] Lemelle.

409
00:26:11.951 --> 00:26:16.378
My daddy's biological mother . . . Her last name was Louis.

410
00:26:16.378 --> 00:26:17.362
Marie Louis.

411
00:26:17.362 --> 00:26:20.805
<v CRAMER>Do you think . . . Is that Louis L-O-U-I-S?

412
00:26:20.805 --> 00:26:21.789
Or L-E-W-I-S?

413
00:26:21.789 --> 00:26:22.281
<v LEMELLE>L-O-U-I-S.

414
00:26:22.281 --> 00:26:25.724
<v CRAMER>Now is there some French in there?

415
00:26:25.724 --> 00:26:26.708
<v LEMELLE>Oh yeah.

416
00:26:26.708 --> 00:26:27.692
All French.

417
00:26:27.692 --> 00:26:30.151
<v CRAMER>From that New Iberia area?

418
00:26:30.151 --> 00:26:30.643
Okay.

419
00:26:30.643 --> 00:26:31.627
<v LEMELLE>New Iberia.

420
00:26:31.627 --> 00:26:36.546
My grandfather was . . . Actually my father was born in Leonsville.

421
00:26:36.546 --> 00:26:38.022
That's around Opelousas.

422
00:26:38.022 --> 00:26:38.514
<v CRAMER>Okay.

423
00:26:38.514 --> 00:26:39.497
<v LEMELLE>Leonsville, Arnauldville.

424
00:26:39.497 --> 00:26:50.319
And I think he left there when he was a kid because my grandfather moved probably when my dad was about seven,

425
00:26:50.319 --> 00:26:53.270
eight years old, his mother died.

426
00:26:53.270 --> 00:27:02.124
Then my grandfather up and left and went to New Iberia and my dad was on his own.

427
00:27:02.124 --> 00:27:04.584
<v CRAMER>What did she die of?

428
00:27:04.584 --> 00:27:06.059
Do you know?

429
00:27:06.059 --> 00:27:08.519
Did they talk about it?

430
00:27:08.519 --> 00:27:09.995
<v LEMELLE>My dad's mother?

431
00:27:09.995 --> 00:27:11.470
Never did know.

432
00:27:11.470 --> 00:27:21.308
Because from what my dad say he can remember . . . That all he could remember was seeing a lot of blood.

433
00:27:21.308 --> 00:27:26.227
So we figure that she had a miscarriage or something.

434
00:27:26.227 --> 00:27:28.686
Back then they didn't know.

435
00:27:28.686 --> 00:27:40.000
He just remembers seeing his mother bleeding, and some of this stuff we can take off what I'm just telling you all.

436
00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:40.514
<v CRAMER>Right.

437
00:27:40.514 --> 00:27:43.596
Well it was very common, unfortunately.

438
00:27:43.596 --> 00:27:46.164
<v LEMELLE>It was very common then.

439
00:27:46.164 --> 00:27:49.760
After that my dad and his brothers,

440
00:27:49.760 --> 00:28:03.116
they kind of had to . . . They were more or less on their own and my father end up going to live on a former slave property,

441
00:28:03.116 --> 00:28:06.199
and they’d taken care of him.

442
00:28:06.199 --> 00:28:13.390
When he was fourteen years old he went to work on an oil platform,

443
00:28:13.390 --> 00:28:23.664
and I'm going to bring you the papers and show you, my father went to work on an oil platform.

444
00:28:23.664 --> 00:28:24.692
<v CRAMER>How old?

445
00:28:24.692 --> 00:28:26.747
<v LEMELLE>When he was fourteen.

446
00:28:26.747 --> 00:28:35.993
My dad was born in [1908?] and he went to work on this platform at fourteen years old.

447
00:28:35.993 --> 00:28:43.185
And the story that we gathered, and I have proof of it, that the

448
00:28:43.185 --> 00:28:50.890
<c.p>owner of the land and the rigs and everything was a Gardener . . . Name was Gardener.

449
00:28:50.890 --> 00:28:59.623
And my dad say one day at payday he had been working and what they would do,

450
00:28:59.623 --> 00:29:08.356
they would take your name down and you come up to the house to get your money.

451
00:29:08.356 --> 00:29:19.658
And the owner happened to see the name Lemelle and she told the foreman to go bring that kid Lemelle to her.

452
00:29:19.658 --> 00:29:23.253
I got the papers at the house.

453
00:29:23.253 --> 00:29:33.527
And when she brought him to the house she discovered that my father's family, Lemelle’s, was in to their family.

454
00:29:33.527 --> 00:29:35.068
From the wars.

455
00:29:35.068 --> 00:29:36.096
François Lemelle.

456
00:29:36.096 --> 00:29:50.479
She kept my dad . . . What they call then was the big house and gave him a responsibility of helping her with her kids and around in the house.

457
00:29:50.479 --> 00:29:53.048
He raised Marie Belle Gardener.

458
00:29:53.048 --> 00:29:56.130
He raised her from a baby.

459
00:29:56.130 --> 00:30:07.945
When she was grown and got married to a David Garrison he followed her and he worked in that family probably seventy years.

460
00:30:07.945 --> 00:30:08.973
True story.

461
00:30:08.973 --> 00:30:10.000
<v CRAMER>Wow.

462
00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:18.893
<v LEMELLE>And I have the papers where François . . . and that's why she brought him out of . . . That's how my dad got out of where he was at.

463
00:30:18.893 --> 00:30:23.339
Through the name and through this people with love and feelings for you.

464
00:30:23.339 --> 00:30:26.417
And he’d taken care of her from a baby.

465
00:30:26.417 --> 00:30:30.863
Then he ended up coming to work for her here in Lake Charles.

466
00:30:30.863 --> 00:30:33.599
Raised her . . . Raised her kids and her grandkids.

467
00:30:33.599 --> 00:30:36.336
<v CRAMER>So that's what brought him this way then?

468
00:30:36.336 --> 00:30:38.388
<v LEMELLE>That's what brought him this way.

469
00:30:38.388 --> 00:30:43.860
That's what freed him from where he was at out on the boardwalks to the house.

470
00:30:43.860 --> 00:30:47.622
You know they got this movie from something to the White

471
00:30:47.622 --> 00:30:47.964
<c.p>House?

472
00:30:47.964 --> 00:30:54.805
My dad went from the . . . He used to tell us he said, "Baby ya’ll don't know what I went through."

473
00:30:54.805 --> 00:31:01.645
He said that actually the people that they sent him to when he was a kid at seven years old,

474
00:31:01.645 --> 00:31:07.459
he said in the winter time people back them had what they called arthritis, or rheumatoid arthritis.

475
00:31:07.459 --> 00:31:13.274
And he said the owner was so mean and they . . . He used to tell him, my dad,

476
00:31:13.274 --> 00:31:25.244
he said the man would take and bring him and tell him to lay down that he could put his feet . . . That the soreness would leave out of him and go into somebody else's body.

477
00:31:25.244 --> 00:31:27.980
That was just old things that they believed.

478
00:31:27.980 --> 00:31:33.111
And he say he was so glad to get away from there he ran off.

479
00:31:33.111 --> 00:31:35.163
At fourteen years old he left.

480
00:31:35.163 --> 00:31:50.554
He stayed there for seven years and he left and that's when he ended up where he was at taking care of this little old girl and ended up this little girl turned up to be his . . . Turned in to be his key to life.

481
00:31:50.554 --> 00:31:51.238
Yes indeed.

482
00:31:51.238 --> 00:31:53.632
I could almost make you cry sometimes.

483
00:31:53.632 --> 00:31:55.000
<v CRAMER>You did already.

484
00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:56.208
<v LEMELLE>Well, don't cry.

485
00:31:56.208 --> 00:31:57.818
Be glad for us.

486
00:31:57.818 --> 00:31:59.429
See like Julia's son?

487
00:31:59.429 --> 00:32:09.896
He wouldn't . . . He would not be where he's at in Paris if it wasn't for the Lemelle name because my . . . The ancestors that came from France,

488
00:32:09.896 --> 00:32:13.922
there was a François Lemelle that had a bakery there.

489
00:32:13.922 --> 00:32:24.792
And when Jeffery left he was Jeffery Gardener and he got there and he said he came home and they asked him could he change his name?

490
00:32:24.792 --> 00:32:31.636
So he put the . . . Added the Lemelle to it and he say, "Man everything start happening good."

491
00:32:31.636 --> 00:32:32.039
Yeah.

492
00:32:32.039 --> 00:32:36.468
<v CRAMER>Well I know that we could do . . . We could keep going.

493
00:32:36.468 --> 00:32:40.091
And I want . . . I really do want to continue . . .

494
00:32:40.091 --> 00:32:41.299
<v LEMELLE>[The questions started?]

495
00:32:41.299 --> 00:32:54.584
<v CRAMER>So we're going to maybe pause it here and then make a plan to come back and do this for a good long time because that was only question number two for me.

496
00:32:54.584 --> 00:32:57.000
<v LEMELLE>How many questions you have?

497
00:32:57.000 --> 00:32:57.973
<v CRAMER>Like thirty.

498
00:32:57.973 --> 00:32:58.946
<v LEMELLE>Question, baby!

499
00:32:58.946 --> 00:33:00.891
<v CRAMER>I tell you what.

500
00:33:00.891 --> 00:33:03.323
In closing just today's up.

501
00:33:03.323 --> 00:33:09.160
What is one thing that you want people to know about Mossville?

502
00:33:09.160 --> 00:33:11.106
Because it's changing rapidly.

503
00:33:11.106 --> 00:33:24.239
I mean even since we started coming in January it has really undergone a lot of changes if you . . . One thing that you want everybody to know,

504
00:33:24.239 --> 00:33:30.562
and the kids who . . . the young kids that are in the community now,

505
00:33:30.562 --> 00:33:37.372
and the people who come in later, what do you want people to know?

506
00:33:37.372 --> 00:33:39.317
<v LEMELLE>According to my history,

507
00:33:39.317 --> 00:33:51.964
I guess to sum it up would be for me to say this was one of the most wonderful places to be born and raised in,

508
00:33:51.964 --> 00:33:58.287
and a community that everybody loved everyone and everyone taking care of everyone.

509
00:33:58.287 --> 00:34:05.097
Since the 1800s up until now, I would say in the last fifteen years,

510
00:34:05.097 --> 00:34:14.825
was a dramatic change because people from other places began to move in here and families . . . Older families died out.

511
00:34:14.825 --> 00:34:19.202
And a lot of family love just disappeared . . . Closeness.

512
00:34:19.202 --> 00:34:25.039
Because you didn't have . . . relatives wasn't necessarily . . . They were important but friends

513
00:34:25.039 --> 00:34:33.795
<c.p>were just as important as relatives because people that you grew up with was like brothers and sisters.

514
00:34:33.795 --> 00:34:38.659
Like sisters and brothers, that's how we grew up here.

515
00:34:38.659 --> 00:34:43.036
And I tell you something else we couldn't do.

516
00:34:43.036 --> 00:34:52.764
One of my best friends lived across the street his name was Wilford Payne . . . Was one of my dearest friends.

517
00:34:52.764 --> 00:35:02.979
If you grew up here and one of your brothers had a girl . . . had a sister you couldn't talk to her.

518
00:35:02.979 --> 00:35:05.897
That's just the way it was.

519
00:35:05.897 --> 00:35:09.302
That was a pact that we made.

520
00:35:09.302 --> 00:35:20.489
Wilford . . . And he had a pretty little sister, but that was just a thing that the way we raised up with each other.

521
00:35:20.489 --> 00:35:21.949
It’s how close.

522
00:35:21.949 --> 00:35:26.326
If you were my brother your sister's my sister.

523
00:35:26.326 --> 00:35:29.245
So we . . . You had a boundary.

524
00:35:29.245 --> 00:35:37.027
So we had to find somebody that wasn't your friend to court the girls. [Laughter] Yeah.

525
00:35:37.027 --> 00:35:38.000
<v CRAMER>Well . . .

526
00:35:38.000 --> 00:35:42.493
<v LEMELLE>Well I have a lot of stories I could tell you about this place baby.

527
00:35:42.493 --> 00:35:58.070
<v CRAMER>I know and I really . . . What I'm thinking is and we can talk about this off tape too is figuring out a way to just come one day stay the night and come back the very next day because there's so much that you have to tell us and that you've retained.

528
00:35:58.070 --> 00:36:09.454
The stories that you listen to when you were growing up that your grandparents told you and just . . . I can guarantee there's a lot of people out there don't really understand things like you were talking about today.

529
00:36:09.454 --> 00:36:13.947
Salted meat, gardening by the moon, making coal . . . Those are the kinds of things that . . .

530
00:36:13.947 --> 00:36:15.744
<v LEMELLE>We did everything by the moon.

531
00:36:15.744 --> 00:36:18.141
Girls, ladies, you had things by the moon.

532
00:36:18.141 --> 00:36:23.233
Grandma knew what your problem was going to be and she told you before it got there.

533
00:36:23.233 --> 00:36:23.533
Girls

534
00:36:23.533 --> 00:36:24.132
<c.p>tighten up.

535
00:36:24.132 --> 00:36:28.026
If it was a certain time of the moon you had a toothache,

536
00:36:28.026 --> 00:36:34.617
you couldn't have a tooth pulled at a certain time because if you pull it at the wrong moon you would bleed.

537
00:36:34.617 --> 00:36:41.207
There was another time that the moon was a certain way you could pull a tooth and it wouldn't bleed at all.

538
00:36:41.207 --> 00:36:46.000
Ladies personal stuff . . . Grandmother could tell you things that you should and should not do.

539
00:36:46.000 --> 00:36:48.319
And they never hid anything from us.

540
00:36:48.319 --> 00:36:51.963
That's one thing I always appreciated about our parents and grandparents.

541
00:36:51.963 --> 00:36:53.951
They never hid anything from you.

542
00:36:53.951 --> 00:37:01.902
Boys you were taught what you should and should not do, and especially at certain times with a girl if you made a mistake.

543
00:37:01.902 --> 00:37:08.196
You just didn't do things because they taught us, and we knew and now the kids don't know anything.

544
00:37:08.196 --> 00:37:09.190
Nobody's teaching them.

545
00:37:09.190 --> 00:37:14.160
What they hear is out on the street but you didn't have that home teaching.

546
00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:19.460
Or you planted . . . My dad would plant certain things you had to plant by the moon.

547
00:37:19.460 --> 00:37:26.086
<v CRAMER>Yeah there were things you planted in a new moon and there were things you planted in a full moon.

548
00:37:26.086 --> 00:37:26.748
I think . . .

549
00:37:26.748 --> 00:37:27.411
<v LEMELLE>New moon.

550
00:37:27.411 --> 00:37:30.393
New moon was when you planted above ground crops.

551
00:37:30.393 --> 00:37:32.380
Waste moon you planted below ground.

552
00:37:32.380 --> 00:37:32.712
<v CRAMER>Gourds.

553
00:37:32.712 --> 00:37:34.037
<v LEMELLE>Such as your turnips.

554
00:37:34.037 --> 00:37:37.681
You didn't plant a turnip and a mustard the same time.

555
00:37:37.681 --> 00:37:40.000
Had to be a different moon.

556
00:37:40.000 --> 00:37:40.306
<v CRAMER>Right.

557
00:37:40.306 --> 00:37:45.816
<v LEMELLE>And I could look at them and I could tell you what kind of moon it is now.

558
00:37:45.816 --> 00:37:47.041
<v CRAMER>What kind of moon?

559
00:37:47.041 --> 00:37:47.959
<v LEMELLE>What's the date?

560
00:37:47.959 --> 00:37:48.571
<v CRAMER>The twenty-fourth.

561
00:37:48.571 --> 00:37:49.796
<v LEMELLE>Today is the twenty-fourth?

562
00:37:49.796 --> 00:37:51.327
This is a September twenty-fourth?

563
00:37:51.327 --> 00:37:55.000
In four days . . . About four days you have a full moon.

564
00:37:55.000 --> 00:37:55.962
<v CRAMER>That's about right.

565
00:37:55.962 --> 00:37:57.886
<v LEMELLE>Because it should be three-quarter full.

566
00:37:57.886 --> 00:37:58.527
<v CRAMER>It's waxing.

567
00:37:58.527 --> 00:37:59.169
That's right.

568
00:37:59.169 --> 00:38:00.772
<v LEMELLE>Because you know why else?

569
00:38:00.772 --> 00:38:03.017
I'm planting my mustard greens next week.

570
00:38:03.017 --> 00:38:03.979
It should be . . .

571
00:38:03.979 --> 00:38:05.903
<v CRAMER>[Laughs] And that's how you knew.

572
00:38:05.903 --> 00:38:08.789
So you still garden by the moon then, too?

573
00:38:08.789 --> 00:38:09.430
<v LEMELLE>Oh, yeah.

574
00:38:09.430 --> 00:38:13.599
Y’all going to have to come get you some vegetables from the house.

575
00:38:13.599 --> 00:38:14.241
<v CRAMER>Oh, yes.

576
00:38:14.241 --> 00:38:14.561
Yes.

577
00:38:14.561 --> 00:38:15.523
Well I have . . .

578
00:38:15.523 --> 00:38:20.654
<v LEMELLE>But whenever you guys are ready just let me know and we going to gather back.

579
00:38:20.654 --> 00:38:20.975
<v CRAMER>Okay.

580
00:38:20.975 --> 00:38:21.295
Alright.

581
00:38:21.295 --> 00:38:22.899
We will definitely do that.

582
00:38:22.899 --> 00:38:28.030
And so this is just the end of session one on a brief oral history day.

583
00:38:28.030 --> 00:38:37.008
And I just wanted to get in there and roll up our sleeves and get started and give you . . . So that we can get some of your story.

584
00:38:37.008 --> 00:38:43.422
And on behalf of the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History and on behalf of the Imperial Calcasieu Museum,

585
00:38:43.422 --> 00:38:49.194
with whom we are partnering, it has been a pleasure and we're very grateful for your time today.

586
00:38:49.194 --> 00:38:49.835
Thank you.

587
00:38:49.835 --> 00:38:51.118
<v LEMELLE>Thank you so much.

588
00:38:51.118 --> 00:38:52.080
No thank you.

589
00:38:52.080 --> 00:38:54.646
What this once was . . . What it once was.

590
00:38:54.646 --> 00:38:55.928
Not what it is.

591
00:38:55.928 --> 00:38:59.776
Not what is was ten years ago, or what it is now.

592
00:38:59.776 --> 00:39:04.266
This used to be a place that you would have loved to been here.

593
00:39:04.266 --> 00:39:07.152
From the elementary school here to the high school.

594
00:39:07.152 --> 00:39:09.076
You'd have loved to been here.

595
00:39:09.076 --> 00:39:10.359
At least, I did.

596
00:39:10.359 --> 00:39:11.000
Goodnight.

597
00:39:11.000 --> 00:39:12.500
<v CRAMER>Well, we love being here now.

598
00:39:12.500 --> 00:39:13.000
<v LEMELLE>Good.

599
00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:13.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4522.

600
00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:13.200
End Session I.]
